g he abandoned his daughter, and it ended up getting settled confidentially. The tabloid avoids the public humiliation, and the celebrity gets a check and the promise of a quiet life. This means that 99% of successful cases are never made public. Third, location matters. U.S. libel laws give tabloids a bit more leeway than you might see in the UK or Australia. If you lived in the UK, you'd see tabloids getting hammered all the time. But even here in the US, there have been some high-profile cases. Melania Trump successfully sued the *Daily Mail* over a story about her immigration history, settling for about $2.9 million. So, while it looks like tabloids have an almost perfect record, the reality is that they are successful not in avoiding libel entirely, but in minimizing damage and keeping the public from finding out when they are wrong.
